WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.360
Good morning, John.

00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:03.480
I'm not going to say I'm a bad writer,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.400
I think I'm a pretty good writer.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:11.120
But I will say that I generally write my best stuff when I'm not doing what people consider writing.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.880
I'm not that interested in metaphor or lyricism or beautiful language.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.560
At least not for their own sakes.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:21.140
I don't tend to think about how words sound beyond rhythm.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.720
I mostly strive to say the most with the least number of words

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.540
and to communicate complicated things as simply as possible

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.680
and usually that serves me very well.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.500
But sometimes, there are just things that can't be said that way

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.160
which often leaves me unable to communicate effectively

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.720
the things that I feel are most important.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:42.580
My dog died.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.040
She got cancer,

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:47.180
osteosarcoma, which is very common in greyhounds.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.680
Nobody knows why.

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:52.120
It almost always metastasises immediately to the lungs

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.980
and the only treatment is amputation, which is painful and short-term.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.120
Many dog owners choose to simply manage the pain

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.020
which is what we did.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.640
And now I'm very sad.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:07.440
I'm sad in a weird way, where I will forget why I'm sad while still being sad

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.740
and I will think: 'Why am I so sad?'

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.240
and then I will remember and feel guilty for having forgotten.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.440
I'm sad in a way where sometimes I won't even have thoughts,

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.920
I will only have sadness and my mind will interject and ask:

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.960
'Why am I crying?' and there will be no reason.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.280
Just the crying.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.100
I don't pretend to have known the mind of my dog.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.140
I will never understand how she thought of me

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.000
or how she understood her own life.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.660
Really, all I can know of my dog is the effect that she had on me.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.340
And that, I suppose, somewhat selfishly, is maybe the important thing?

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.060
Lemon...

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.300
was a dog and she was my dog

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.760
and she was special and valuable and amazing

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.520
but also not any of those things.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:56.860
In every way, she was just another dog.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:58.680
She wasn't a treasure,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.700
but she was my treasure.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:05.080
We all get so caught up in living in such a big world

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.780
with oversized people making their mark,

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.420
and maybe we think that in order to be valuable,

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:11.900
we have to be like that.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:16.180
Even if the mark we make is made of dirt, ugly and easily wiped away,

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.680
we should be making a mark as well.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.520
But, look, Lemon was a dog

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.320
Just another dog.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.420
Lots of people liked her, but she was just a dog.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.220
But, damn it, if I didn't think she was important.

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.680
Damn it if she hasn't left a hole in my heart.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.160
Nothing matters but to each other.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:37.280
And Lemon mattered to me.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.400
People always asked me when meeting Lemon, if she was a rescue.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:46.080
I was always kind of uncomfortable saying yes,

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.620
even though I guess it's technically true.

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:51.340
But it's not as if I got her as an act of goodwill.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.520
We adopted Lemon because we wanted a dog,

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:55.840
and because greyhounds are good dogs.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.820
They're relaxed and loving and adorable.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.520
How could we consider this an act of charity?

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.700
Buying for a small fee a cuddle companion and a reason to get out of bed on Sunday mornings

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:07.500
and somebody to blame your farts on.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:10.360
We didn't rescue Lemon, we just got a dog.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.480
But I didn't really get what was in it for me

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.000
Because as much as her life got better after she was adopted,

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.380
after living life, not abused, but more as livestock than a pet,

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:22.840
my life must have gotten better than her's.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:27.460
Because in valuing Lemon, noble and ridiculous and just another dog,

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.600
I learned that feeling valued and knowing that you matter

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:36.560
is much more about how you think of others than about how others think of you.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.700
And that's just one of the thousand things that that dumb dog taught me and...

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.260
and I guess is still teaching me.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:47.180
Thanks, Lemon.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:49.180
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

